  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n\n- $[-4,-1]$\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[-1,4]$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\"",
